NOTE supplementary-02. Machine draft, cleaned and checked by ear; see docs/transcripts.md.

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.472
<v Orlando>Welcome once again to <lang pt-BR>Tá Falado</lang>.

00:00:02.472 --> 00:00:06.930
<v Orlando>We're here as part of BrazilPod, the Brazilian
Portuguese pronunciation lessons for speakers of Spanish,

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:14.750
<v Orlando>and this comes to you from the
University of Texas here in Austin.

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:19.592
<v Orlando>We would like to welcome everybody to
the supplementary lesson number two.

00:00:19.592 --> 00:00:23.777
<v Orlando>This is simply just a chance to hear
all the Portuguese consonant sounds.

00:00:23.777 --> 00:00:29.739
<v Orlando>It won't be a dialogue or anything exciting like that,
just a chance to hear the words and follow along.

00:00:29.739 --> 00:00:35.300
<v Orlando>So we invite everybody to go to the PDF file,
download it, so you can follow along and read this.

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:37.768
<v Orlando>So we're just going to give you some
of the sounds of Portuguese consonants.

00:00:37.768 --> 00:00:39.550
<v Orlando>And I believe Valdo is starting this off, is that right?

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:40.800
<v Valdo>Yes.

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:46.300
<v Valdo>So we have "p" as in <lang pt-BR>pata, capa</lang>.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:48.950
<v Orlando>And that's similar to English, just we don't have the aspiration.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.300
<v Valdo>Exactly.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:53.300
<v Valdo>We have "b" as in <lang pt-BR>bala, bobo</lang>.

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:57.000
<v Orlando>And that one's also similar to English.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.550
<v Valdo>We have "t" as in <lang pt-BR>tapa, tatu, até</lang>.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:05.010
<v Orlando>And that's just like English without the aspiration.

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:08.000
<v Orlando>And in Portuguese, that could be either dental or alveolar.

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:14.300
<v Michelle>"d" as in <lang pt-BR>data, dado</lang>.

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:17.500
<v Orlando>which is also similar to the English,
but it may be dental or alveolar.

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:23.500
<v Michelle>"k" as in <lang pt-BR>capa, vaca, chique</lang>.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.750
<v Orlando>which is also similar to English without the aspiration.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:31.180
<v Michelle>"g" as in <lang pt-BR>gata, água</lang>.

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:32.500
<v Orlando>Similar to English.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.000
<v Valdo>"tch" as in <lang pt-BR>tia, tente</lang>.

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:43.000
<v Orlando>That's the palatalization of our T, which is
typical of the southeast portion of Brazil.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:47.050
<v Valdo>"dj" as in <lang pt-BR>dia, desde</lang>.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:52.250
<v Orlando>Also the palatalization of the D,
which is typical of Southeast Brazil.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:58.680
<v Michelle>"f" as in <lang pt-BR>fica, esforço</lang>.

00:01:58.850 --> 00:01:59.750
<v Orlando>Just like English?

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:04.800
<v Michelle>"v" as in <lang pt-BR>vaca, avô</lang>.

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:06.300
<v Orlando>just like English.

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:14.550
<v Michelle>"s" as in <lang pt-BR>sala, massa, caça, está, rapaz</lang>.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:15.750
<v Orlando>Just like English.

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:22.050
<v Valdo>"z" as in <lang pt-BR>zero, casa, Lisboa</lang>.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:23.250
<v Orlando>just like English.

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:31.500
<v Valdo>"sh" as in <lang pt-BR>chá, acha, está, rapaz</lang>.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:37.940
<v Orlando>And so that's a CH anywhere, and then in certain
dialects it'll be if a word ends with an S or a Z.

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:48.000
<v Valdo>"zh" as in <lang pt-BR>já, seja, geral, gibi, Lisboa</lang>.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:52.360
<v Orlando>And that's with a J, G-I, G-E, anywhere.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:55.940
<v Orlando>And then in some dialects, if it's
an S or a Z at the end of a syllable.

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:03.800
<v Valdo>"r" as in <lang pt-BR>Roma, carro, amor, carta</lang>.

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:07.500
<v Orlando>So that's the R sound, which is a voiceless velar fricative.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:14.500
<v Valdo>"r" as in <lang pt-BR>Roma, carro, amor, carta</lang>.

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:19.500
<v Orlando>And that's the voiceless glottal fricative of the R sounds.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:25.050
<v Michelle>"r" as in <lang pt-BR>amor, carta</lang>.

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:29.000
<v Orlando>And that's the voiced retroflex
alveolar, another variation of an R.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:36.800
<v Michelle>"r" as in <lang pt-BR>Roma, carro, amor, carta</lang>.

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:39.500
<v Orlando>And that's the alveolar trill, another version of an R.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:47.050
<v Valdo>"r" as in <lang pt-BR>carga, amarga</lang>.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.860
<v Orlando>And that's our R at the end of a
syllable, the voiced velar fricative.

00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:57.050
<v Valdo>And we have "r" as in <lang pt-BR>carga, amarga</lang>.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:02.500
<v Orlando>Another variation of an R at the end of the
syllable. That's the voiced glottal fricative.

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:10.520
<v Michelle>"r" as in <lang pt-BR>para, branca, amor, carta</lang>.

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:12.250
<v Orlando>And that's the alveolar tap.

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:18.550
<v Michelle>"m" as in <lang pt-BR>mala, ama</lang>.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:20.000
<v Orlando>That's similar to English.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:24.200
<v Michelle>"n" as in <lang pt-BR>nada, Ana</lang>.

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:26.550
<v Orlando>And that's similar to English.

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:30.800
<v Michelle>"nh" as in <lang pt-BR>senhor, banho</lang>.

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:35.250
<v Orlando>And that's our palatal nasal or our
palatalized palatal approximant.

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:41.300
<v Valdo>"l" as in <lang pt-BR>lata, bala</lang>.

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:43.300
<v Orlando>That L is just like in English.

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:49.300
<v Valdo>We have "u" as in <lang pt-BR>sal, calma, Brasil</lang>.

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:53.500
<v Orlando>And that's our L at the end of a syllable. Sounds more like a W.

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:58.550
<v Valdo>We have "lh" as in <lang pt-BR>abelha, velha</lang>.

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:04.000
<v Orlando>And that's our LH, which is a palatalized L or a lateral L.

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:11.045
<v Orlando>Okay, and with that then, we'll let everybody get the
PDF file, download and take a listen to it, compare that,

00:05:11.045 --> 00:05:13.203
<v Orlando>and then after they've listened to all that,

